> Author: Linus Torvalds <torvalds@linux-foundation.org>
> Date: =C2=A0 Sat Aug 14 11:44:56 2010 -0700
>
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0mm: fix page table unmap for stack guard page properly
>
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0We do in fact need to unmap the page table _before_ doing th=
e whole
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0stack guard page logic, because if it is needed (mainly 32-b=
it x86 with
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0PAE and CONFIG_HIGHPTE, but other architectures may use it t=
oo) then it
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0will do a kmap_atomic/kunmap_atomic.
>
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0And those kmaps will create an atomic region that we cannot =
do
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0allocations in. =C2=A0However, the whole stack expand code w=
ill need to do
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0anon_vma_prepare() and vma_lock_anon_vma() and they cannot d=
o that in an
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0atomic region.
>
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0Now, a better model might actually be to do the anon_vma_pre=
pare() when
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0_creating_ a VM_GROWSDOWN segment, and not have to worry abo=
ut any of
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0this at page fault time. =C2=A0But in the meantime, this is =
the
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0straightforward fix for the issue.
>
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0See https://bugzilla.kernel.org/show_bug.cgi?id=3D16588 for =
details.
